Here's the full audit report.

---

## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `SerializerTests.cs`

**Summary: 5 Critical · 1 Medium · 1 Low**

This suite is dominated by **self-referential / tautological assertions**. Five of eight tests assert `output == input` on operations that either (a) are expected to transform data, or (b) are given pre-transformed input so the assertion is trivially true. These tests inflate coverage while verifying nothing meaningful.

---

### 🔴 Critical — False Confidence

#### 1. `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` (line 73–77) — Self-referential assertion

Input `"HELLO"` is already uppercase. `"HELLO".ToUpper()` is a no-op, so `Assert.AreEqual(input, result)` is trivially true.

```csharp
// BEFORE — always passes, proves nothing
var input = "HELLO";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// AFTER — actually verifies the transformation
var input = "hello";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

#### 2. `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` (line 39–41) — Self-referential assertion

Input `"alice"` is already lowercase and trimmed. `Normalize()` calls `.Trim().ToLowerInvariant()`, making this a no-op. The test never verifies that normalization *does* anything.

```csharp
// BEFORE — identity case, trivially true
var name = "alice";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);

// AFTER — verify actual transformation
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize("  ALICE  ");
Assert.AreEqual("alice", result);
```

#### 3. `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` (line 58–60) — Self-referential assertion (field vs. itself)

`GetDisplayName()` returns `Name` verbatim. The test asserts `user.Name == display`, which is `user.Name == user.Name`. If `GetDisplayName` were later changed to format the name (e.g., `"Alice (30)"`), this test would *start failing* instead of catching a missing format — the assertion is backwards.

```csharp
// BEFORE — asserts a field against itself
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);

// AFTER — assert against an independent expected value
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);  // explicit expectation
```

#### 4. `Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsSameInput` (line 66–68) — Self-referential assertion

`ValidateEmail` returns its input on success. `Assert.AreEqual(input, result)` will always be true for any valid email. The test never verifies that *invalid* input throws, which is the validator's real purpose.

```csharp
// BEFORE — only tests the trivial pass-through
var input = "hello@world.com";
var result = Validator.ValidateEmail(input);
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// AFTER — test the actual validation behavior
Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(
    () => Validator.ValidateEmail("not-an-email"));
Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(
    () => Validator.ValidateEmail(""));
// Happy path still useful with a literal expected value:
Assert.AreEqual("hello@world.com", Validator.ValidateEmail("hello@world.com"));
```

#### 5. `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` (line 47–53) — Tautological round-trip on a lossy mapper

The production `Mapper` is *lossy by design*: `ToDto` drops `Id` and `Email`, hardcodes `Age = 0`; `ToEntity` drops `Id` (sets 0) and `Email` (sets `""`). A round-trip **cannot** preserve the original entity. Either this test fails at line 50 (`AreEqual(1, 0)`) — meaning it was never run — or it masks silent data loss. Either way it provides false confidence about data preservation.

```csharp
// BEFORE — claims round-trip fidelity that doesn't exist
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Id, backToEntity.Id);       // 1 vs 0
Assert.AreEqual(entity.FullName, backToEntity.FullName);
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Email, backToEntity.Email);  // "a@b.com" vs ""

// AFTER — test each direction independently with explicit expectations
[TestMethod]
public void ToDto_MapsFullNameToName()
{
    var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
    var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, dto.Age);  // document the known lossy behavior
}

[TestMethod]
public void ToEntity_MapsNameToFullName()
{
    var dto = new UserDto { Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 30 };
    var entity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", entity.FullName);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, entity.Id);    // document known defaults
    Assert.AreEqual("", entity.Email);
}
```

---

### 🟡 Medium

#### 6. `Clone_ProducesEqualObject` (line 29–33) — Missing reference-inequality assertion

The test verifies property values match but never asserts the clone is a *different object*. If `Clone()` returned `this`, the test would still pass.

```csharp
// ADD after existing assertions:
Assert.AreNotSame(original, clone);
```

---

### 🔵 Low

#### 7. Test class naming — `SerializerTests` is a grab-bag

The class tests `Serializer`, `Money`, `Config`, `NameNormalizer`, `Mapper`, `Validator`, and `string.ToUpper`. A single class covering six unrelated types makes failures harder to triage. Consider splitting by SUT.

